+- Status: done. Verdict: **not adopted** (rule failed: max 3.4x lower, not 10x; the host's own stalls set the floor). Every other criterion held.
+- Slice counts, checked against ghost.go: slices are Horizon/2 (`sliceLen = (H+1)/2`); an entry of slice sl is dead by slice sl+3 (for odd H, from the last tick of slice sl+2); decoded with 2 bits against epoch cur it reads correctly anywhere in [cur−3, cur] and aliases to cur (live) at sl = cur−4. `advance` today clears every entry with real + 2 < ns, so at epoch cur no entry is older than cur−2; the slack is one slice.
+- Design (before any reading; `lab-e11-incremental-sweep.patch`): the per-word clearing in `advance` becomes a helper. During slice cur, every `add` sweeps the next 4 buckets of a shared cursor, clearing entries with real + 2 < cur (dead since this slice began), each word committed only while the epoch is the one it decoded against (add's own rule). A one-slice move (cur → cur+1) under `g.mu` claims the buckets the cursor hasn't reached, sweeps them, waits for adds still sweeping claimed ones, then moves the epoch and resets the cursor: O(1) when the adds kept up. A move of more than one slice (an idle gap) also runs today's full sweep. Invariant kept: at epoch cur no entry is older than cur−3, so none is decoded where its slice aliases. Answers are unchanged where the adds keep up (entries of cur−3 decode as dead either way).
