+### E13: `Merge` — candidate
+
+**What it is.** A new method (`lab-e13-merge.patch`: `go/merge.go`, plus its test `go/lab_merge_test.go`; 181 added lines; no existing file changed):
+
+```go
+// Merge adds g's keys to f: for a key in both, the two registers are
+// re-bucketed to the newer one's epoch and summed bucket by bucket (an
+// overflow sets the saturated bit), and the last touch is the newer one; a
+// key only g holds goes in as a first touch would (which may evict).
+func (f *Filter) Merge(g *Filter) error
+```
+
+**How it works.**
+- Checks first, returning an error and changing nothing: `f == g`; a different shape (lines, horizon, unit, resolution); ConstantTime or Background on either side; hot entries in use on either side.
+- `now` for expiry is the newest last touch in either table (a scan of both).
+- For each live slot of g (skipping empty, busy and expired ones): its identity (primary line, 16-bit fingerprint, 12-bit tag) is turned back into a hash with the same line, fingerprint and tag in f (the line part is ⌈line·2³⁶/lines⌉), and the round trip is checked (`primary`, `fingerprint` and `tag` of the new hash must give the identity back, else an error).
+- Under the key's claim stripe: if f holds the key, both registers are read as views, re-bucketed to the newer epoch with `register.advance`, summed with `register.add` (spilling to older buckets as a touch does), the saturated bit set on overflow or if either side had it, and word0 keeps f's fingerprint and displaced bit with the newer last touch. Else the slot goes in through `claim` (the first-touch path: free slot, cuckoo path, or eviction to f's stash), with g's register and last touch.
+
+**Every criterion, with its reading** (`TF_LAB=1 go test -count=1 -run '^TestLabMerge$' -v .`; deterministic; tfbench's velocity workload, 2,000 keys split at random between A and B, S fed everything, M = Merge(A) + Merge(B) at 47 checkpoints, 470,000 comparisons; evictions 0 in A, B, S and M):
+
+| Criterion (written before the reading) | Reading | Met |
+|---|---|---|
+| Seen identical to the single filter wherever neither evicted | 0 differences in 470,000 | yes |
+| Count MAE at most 1.5x the single filter's, every window | 1 s 0.2841 vs 0.2841 (1.000); 5 s 0.0014 vs 0.0014 (0.992); 15 s 0.0188 vs 0.0189 (0.993); 30 s 0.0408 vs 0.0408 (0.999); 60 s 0.0233 vs 0.0233 (0.998) | yes |
+| No unflagged answer with Upper below the truth | 0 unflagged; 12 flagged Saturated (hot entries were off, so some buckets overflowed, in M and in S alike) | yes |
+| FPR (reported) | 1M absent probes: false Seen 0 merged, 0 single | — |
+
+Also: gates on HEAD + patch clean (sanity PASS, digest identical, `tfbench -exact` identical, since nothing calls `Merge`); `go test -race -run '^TestLabMerge$'` PASS.
+
+**Reproduce.**
+
+```
+cd go
+git apply verify/experiments/lab-e13-merge.patch   # from a checkout of go/ (in a subdirectory of the repo use patch -p1)
+TF_LAB=1 go test -count=1 -run '^TestLabMerge$' -v .
+```
+
+**Limits, before it could ship.**
+- Hot entries are refused: a key promoted to a hot entry keeps 32-bit exact counters that a register can't hold. Merging them needs entry-to-entry sums (and an entry in f for a key hot only in g).
+- g's stash and overflow table are not merged: histories g had evicted are dropped from the merged answer (their keys read as never seen in f, where g would have restored them).
+- Both filters must be quiescent; nothing guards against concurrent calls during a merge.
+- If `claim` keeps losing races it gives up on that key after 8 tries without an error. In a quiescent filter `claim` returns a slot or evicts, so this is not expected to happen, but it is not reported if it does.
+- Keys that `claim` evicts from f when it is full go to f's stash, as any first touch would; the test never filled f, so that path is unmeasured here.
+- Only the velocity workload was tested; flood traffic, ConstantTime and Background are untested or refused.
